{"plan_objective_id": 1, "updated_plan_objective_json": "{
  "id": 1,
  "objective": "Find and gather evidence on cost savings and ROI of open-source scientific hardware (Pearce's work)",
  "rationale": "Core part of the question - need quantitative data on cost savings from open-source lab equipment",
  "status": "completed",
  "result": "Found extensive evidence: average savings of 87% across FOSH devices, 89% with Arduino, 92% with 3D printing, 94% combined. ROI of 460%-8,300% for syringe pump case study. Education optics: 96.7% savings. Scaling could save >$1 billion globally.",
  "evaluation": "Multiple quantitative datapoints from Pearce 2015, 2016, 2020 papers with detailed cost comparisons"
}"}

{"plan_objective_id": 2, "updated_plan_objective_json": "{
  "id": 2,
  "objective": "Find studies on business models and commercial viability of open hardware companies",
  "rationale": "Need to understand how open hardware companies monetize and their financial outcomes",
  "status": "completed",
  "result": "Found data on: Arduino (200 boards 2005 to 150k+ by 2010, trademark licensing 10% of revenue), SparkFun ($846K in 2005 to $7M in 2008, up to ~$75M cumulative), MakerBot (acquired $403M, then declined after going proprietary), Aleph Objects/LulzBot (2,782% 3-yr growth, $5.8M Q2 2016, Inc 500 #122), Prusa Research (~CZK 6.6B combined revenue 2024, 1000+ employees, 300K orders/year). Business models include: kit sales, specialty components, consulting, trademark licensing, subscriptions, training.",
  "evaluation": "Good coverage of company-specific data with quantitative revenue, growth, and strategic details"
}"}

{"plan_objective_id": 3, "updated_plan_objective_json": "{
  "id": 3,
  "objective": "Find information on open hardware licensing (CERN OHL) and OSHWA certification trends",
  "rationale": "Legal and institutional frameworks are key to commercialization",
  "status": "completed",
  "result": "CERN OHL: published 2011, based on TAPR OHL, drafted by Javier Serrano and Myriam Ayass. Three variants: strongly reciprocal (OHL-S), weakly reciprocal (OHL-W), permissive (OHL-P). OSHWA certification: 977 projects Aug 2020, 1,601 by Jul 2021 (64% growth in ~1 year).",
  "evaluation": "Covers license history, structure, and certification statistics"
}"}

{"plan_objective_id": 4, "updated_plan_objective_json": "{
  "id": 4,
  "objective": "Find comparisons between open-source hardware and open-source software commercialization models",
  "rationale": "Understanding similarities and differences helps contextualize OSH economics",
  "status": "completed",
  "result": "Key similarities: community-based development, service models (Red Hat analogy at $1.79B/yr), complementary market strategies. Key differences: hardware has manufacturing costs, distribution costs, compliance requirements (EMC, CE, FCC), weaker licensing (copyright vs patents), more heterogeneous tools. 96% of OSH companies use highly restrictive licenses. 86% of OSH market offers depend on OSS.",
  "evaluation": "Good coverage from multiple sources including Ferreira 2010, Lock 2013, Parker 2021, Mies 2024"
}"}

{"plan_objective_id": 5, "updated_plan_objective_json": "{
  "id": 5,
  "objective": "Gather detailed evidence from retrieved papers and synthesize findings",
  "rationale": "Need to extract quantitative data and specific findings from papers found",
  "status": "completed",
  "result": "All key data gathered across cost savings, business models, licensing, and comparisons",
  "evaluation": "54 pieces of evidence gathered from 25+ relevant papers covering all topics"
}"}

{"plan_objective_id": 6, "updated_plan_objective_json": "{
  "id": 6,
  "objective": "Create summary artifacts and submit final answer",
  "rationale": "Compile findings into a well-organized answer with citations",
  "status": "in-progress",
  "result": null,
  "evaluation": null
}"}

